  "metadata": {
    "name": "Shared bus",
    "createdAt": "2026-11-21T12:00:00.000Z",
    "modifiedAt": "2026-11-21T12:00:00.000Z",
    "notes": "Two 8-bit sources, A and B, share one wire, the bus. Each reaches it through a tri-state buffer, which drives its output only while its enable is on, and otherwise lets go of the wire.\n\nWith neither enable on, nothing drives the bus: it floats, drawn dotted, and Bus reads Z. Turn Enable A on and Bus shows A. Select A and type a value, such as 0x5A, to see it follow.\n\nNow turn Enable B on as well. Where A and B differ, two drivers pull the wire opposite ways, and nobody can say what it carries: those bits are X, the bus is drawn dashed, and Bus shows X for each hex digit that holds one. Make B equal to A and the conflict is gone. This is how a computer puts many parts on one bus: every part has a buffer, and only one is enabled at a time."
  },
